    body: `
      <div class="section-heading">Background</div>
      <p>The Armed Forces Research Institute of Medical Sciences (AFRIMS) began in 1959 as the Thailand Southeast Asia Treaty Organization (SEATO) Cholera Research Project, established to respond to severe regional cholera outbreaks. In 1961, it became the SEATO Medical Research Laboratory, expanding its work to a broad range of tropical and military-relevant diseases and supporting US efforts during the Vietnam War. Following SEATO's dissolution in 1977, the institute was restructured under US–Thai bilateral agreements and renamed AFRIMS. Over six decades, it has grown into the largest US Army overseas medical research laboratory, conducting research on tropical diseases, supporting public health and military readiness, and collaborating closely with Thai medical authorities[89].</p>

      <div class="section-heading">Emergence of Allegations</div>
      <p>Allegations targeting AFRIMS emerged during the COVID_19 pandemic within a digital environment already saturated by the global US "biolab" narrative. Thai-language material portrayed AFRIMS as part of a worldwide network of US Army laboratories allegedly linked to the origins of COVID-19[90]. Posts and screenshots asserted that the virus originated from Fort Detrick in the United States — a facility long featured in Russian and Soviet-era biological disinformation — and then mapped that foundational narrative onto AFRIMS as Thailand's local node in the same supposed system[91].</p>
      <p>Within this framing, AFRIMS was described as "the US Army's largest pathogen laboratory,"[92] implying that Thailand hosted a major hub of American military-biological activity. This positioning mirrored disinformation campaigns against US-linked laboratories in Georgia, Ukraine and Africa, where overseas research sites were portrayed as part of a single transnational network. AFRIMS was accused of conducting high-risk experiments, secretly exporting Thai biological samples, and operating under unsafe biosafety conditions[93]. More sensational claims alleged unethical human experimentation and covert transport of pathogens by US[94]. Much of the content originated from an anonymous "whistleblower" document that blended personal grievances, misinterpretations of laboratory practice and tropes drawn from international biolab conspiracies. As in similar materials used in other regions, it contained no verifiable evidence but reproduced the narrative architecture of U.S.-biolab disinformation almost verbatim[95]. These motifs are standard components of the global biolab narrative and reappear almost unchanged across countries.</p>

      <div class="section-heading">Narrative Amplification</div>
      <p>Until 2021, AFRIMS attracted little controversy in Thailand[97]. However, the COVID-19 pandemic created a moment of heightened sensitivity around foreign-funded laboratories and pathogen research scrutiny, making the institute vulnerable to being drawn into the lowest tier of the Matryoshka ecosystem. The allegations circulated primarily through fringe Facebook pages, Telegram channels, pseudonymous blogs and small YouTube accounts. These spaces recycled and translated the anonymous document, presenting it as insider proof of hidden American activity.</p>
      <p>Mainstream Thai institutions intervened quickly. Major outlets and the Royal Thai Army issued public rebuttals, while the Anti-Fake News Center formally debunked the claims[98]. This top-down rebuttal prevented the narrative from moving into the middle tiers of national political or media discourse, keeping it confined to online fringe spaces. Nevertheless, the narrative did not remain entirely isolated. Russian state-linked messaging indirectly gestured toward AFRIMS when a TASS article quoted Igor Kirillov describing a large U.S. Army biological research unit in Thailand with hundreds of staff, generating an unmistakable reference without naming the institute. This ambiguity is characteristic of mid-tier Matryoshka signalling — the target is implied in a way that allows deniability while still embedding Thailand within the global biolab frame.</p>

      <div class="section-heading">Matryoshka Model</div>
      <p>Although the AFRIMS allegations never achieved mainstream traction, their structure fits the lowest tier of the Matryoshka architecture. The anonymous document and fringe-platform narratives functioned as micro-level reproductions of the foundational biolab story, translated into Thai political and cultural idioms.</p>
      <p>Because such content persists online, it remains available for selective appropriation by higher-tier actors. As seen in the TASS reference, even marginal claims can be lifted upward and incorporated into state-level narratives when strategically useful, reinforcing the cyclical logic of the model.</p>

      <div class="section-heading">Why the Narrative Resonates</div>
      <p>The AFRIMS narrative resonated in small segments of Thailand's online community because it aligned with pandemic-era fear, longstanding sensitivities around US military presence, and widespread uncertainty about how biosurveillance and pathogen research operate. Claims about secret experiments, foreign sample extraction and unethical research mirror themes already present in anti-US, anti-vaccine and anti-elite online spaces. By providing a coherent storyline linking COVID-19 to foreign laboratories, the narrative offered emotional and political meaning during a period of uncertainty. Although it did not become nationally influential, the AFRIMS case shows how even low-credibility, bottom-tier narratives remain geopolitically useful when they align with global disinformation templates.</p>

      <div class="section-heading">Key Takeaways</div>
      <div class="takeaway-list">
        <div class="takeaway"><strong>Narrative Pathway:</strong> AFRIMS reflects a bottom-tier Matryoshka dynamic, in which fringe local narratives can be selectively incorporated into higher-tier state messaging.</div>
        <div class="takeaway"><strong>Regional Resonance:</strong> Pandemic fear, sensitivity to US military presence and existing conspiracy cultures enabled the narrative to take root in limited communities.</div>
        <div class="takeaway"><strong>Local Vulnerabilities:</strong> AFRIMS' US Army affiliation, historical regional footprint and role in pathogen surveillance allowed routine scientific activity to be reframed as covert military research.</div>
      </div>`,
