    body: `
      <div class="section-heading">Background</div>
      <p>In mid-2023, reports emerged from local Haitian media[122] of an anthrax outbreak, prompting concern in the Dominican Republic and across the Caribbean. Health authorities linked cases to the consumption and handling of meat[123] from animals that had died suddenly. Several individuals were hospitalised and a small number of deaths were reported[124]. While some cases were laboratory-confirmed, the outbreak remained under investigation and the full transmission chain was not definitively established.</p>
      <p>Dominican authorities, concerned about cross-border spread, enacted preventive health and security measures at key border crossings such as El Carrizal and Dajabón[125]. Given the volume of informal trade and daily movement between Haiti and the Dominican Republic, these measures were deemed legitimate public-health responses.</p>
      <p>However, this outbreak entered an information environment already conditioned by the global "biolab" narrative. As news circulated online,[126] unverified claims began circulating that an infected Haitian individual intended to cross the border to "deliberately spread"[127] anthrax as a biological weapon. The claim did not draw on medical evidence but on the global repertoire of bioterrorism imagery that often accompanies disease outbreaks.</p>
      <p>Although the rumour circulated mainly through fringe digital platforms and informal social-media networks, it demonstrated how a foundational bioweapon frame can rapidly attach itself to routine public-health events.</p>

      <div class="section-heading">Escalation and Official Response</div>
      <p>In response to the rumour, the Dominican Ministry of Defense and Ministry of Public Health launched an investigation into the alleged "bioweapon carrier"[128]. Field teams were deployed and international partners, including the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC) and the Pan American Health Organisation (PAHO), were consulted. The Ministry of Public Health issued an official statement confirming that the claim was false. The Haitian citizen had been examined, including at the Air Force Hospital, and no anthrax infection was found. There was no evidence of deliberate spread or bioterrorism.</p>
      <p>At the same time, legitimate precautionary measures such as restrictions on meat and agricultural imports from Haiti[129] were implemented to mitigate real zoonotic risk. These public-health actions were then misread by some audiences as confirmation that something sinister had occurred, illustrating how official responses can be absorbed into disinformation ecosystems.</p>
      <p>Although the false bioweapon rumour was not the primary driver, it contributed to a broader atmosphere of insecurity. Haitian traders protested and blocked border crossings, halting trade and inflaming political tensions. Tourism and border economies experienced short-term disruption at a politically sensitive moment, coinciding with the inauguration of the Dominican Republic's new president. In this context, biosecurity narratives carried symbolic weight, shaping perceptions of state competence and national vulnerability.</p>

      <div class="section-heading">Matryoshka Model</div>
      <p>The anthrax rumour exemplifies a lowest-tier Matryoshka dynamic. At the innermost tier, community-level fears about disease, food safety, migration and cross-border health risks generated a rumour that an infected person was being used as a biological weapon. This narrative did not arise independently—it drew on the same global bioterrorism and biolab frames circulating internationally.</p>
      <p>At the intermediate tier, official investigations, border measures and health advisories became incorporated into the narrative as perceived confirmation.</p>
      <p>At the outer tier, the foundational global biolab narrative provided the interpretive framework that made the rumour intelligible amidst spiralling anxieties relating to disease outbreaks and border control.</p>
      <p>Even without direct Russian amplification, the architecture of the Matryoshka model was present: global narratives shaped local interpretation, local reaction created apparent evidence, and official responses became part of the story.</p>

      <div class="section-heading">Why the Narrative Resonated</div>
      <p>The rumour resonated because it aligned with deep anxieties about migration, poverty, food safety and disease transmission along the Haiti–Dominican Republic border. Anthrax, a disease already associated with bioterrorism in global popular culture, made the claim emotionally powerful. In a region shaped by inequality and institutional fragility, the idea that disease could be weaponised felt intuitively plausible, even in the absence of evidence. The case shows that disinformation does not require foreign instigation to operate; global narrative templates are now locally internalised.</p>

      <div class="section-heading">Key Takeaways</div>
      <div class="takeaway-list">
        <div class="takeaway"><strong>Narrative Pathway:</strong> The anthrax case reflects a bottom-tier Matryoshka dynamic, in which local rumours draw on global bioweapon narratives and gain traction through official response.</div>
        <div class="takeaway"><strong>Regional Resonance:</strong> Cross-border migration, food insecurity and historical mistrust made the rumour emotionally persuasive.</div>
        <div class="takeaway"><strong>Local Vulnerabilities:</strong> Limited trust, informal trade networks and high disease anxiety allowed a false bioterror frame to attach to a real outbreak.</div>
      </div>`,
