    body: `
      <p>International cooperation has been critical to building Africa's public health and biosecurity infrastructure. Surveillance partnerships, laboratory upgrades, genomic sequencing efforts and epidemic-response training, all of which have been prominently supported by Global Partnership states, have strengthened outbreak detection and response across West, Central and East Africa. Yet these same collaborations have become recurrent targets of strategic biological disinformation.</p>
      <p>In Africa, biological disinformation has become a persistent and expanding feature of the information environment, thriving in digital and analogue spaces alike as social media platforms, messaging apps and community networks rapidly amplify rumours and falsehoods. Documented disinformation campaigns targeting African publics exploit deep legacies of inequitable scientific exchange, political fault lines and rising internet connectivity to cast health security cooperation as exploitative or harmful, undermining trust in outbreak responses and routine health interventions. These dynamics are compounded by evolving foreign influence operations and algorithm-driven echo chambers that mix sensationalist content with local anxieties.</p>
      <p>Facilities such as the Kenema Government Hospital in Sierra Leone, the Rodolphe Mérieux Laboratory in Goma, and the Kenya Medical Research Institute (KEMRI) have repeatedly and falsely been portrayed as covert Western biological weapons laboratories. These claims rarely rest on evidence but instead draw plausibility from asymmetries in global scientific cooperation, longstanding suspicions of external influence and the enduring legacy of colonial medical abuse, which are then mobilised to give the narratives local credibility.</p>
      <p>The narratives operate through a Matryoshka-style, tiered architecture with direct implications for biological arms control. In the smallest, innermost tier, community-level fears, which are often rooted in previous outbreaks and understandable medical mistrust, generate rumours that are emotionally resonant and highly adaptive. These bottom-up narratives do not originate in isolation but are interpreted through the foundational biolab frame circulating at higher levels. At the intermediate tier, national-level actors, including journalists, political intermediaries and activist networks, rearticulate and politicise these claims, embedding them in domestic debates about sovereignty, corruption and foreign influence. At the outermost tier, international state-linked messaging, most prominently from Russian defence and foreign-policy actors, codifies and legitimises these localised stories by integrating them into a broader geopolitical narrative of clandestine US military–biological activities. High-level narratives provide interpretive authority, national discourse supplies political meaning, and local rumours generate the emotive "evidence" that sustains the system.</p>
      <p>While the foundational "biolab" narrative is global in design, its expression in Africa reflects distinct regional dynamics. In Sierra Leone, for example, disinformation surrounding Kenema fused with local grievances during the Ebola epidemic, transforming the death of Dr Sheikh Umarr Khan into "evidence" of a neo-colonial plot. In the Democratic Republic of the Congo, narratives circulating during the 2019–22 Ebola outbreak portrayed international responders as agents of exploitation, enabling conspiracy theories about Western extermination campaigns, organ theft, and population control. At KEMRI in Kenya, Russian diplomatic messaging and state media layered the global biolab narrative onto dormant anxieties by framing long-standing US-funded health collaborations as opaque, militarised or in violation of national sovereignty.</p>
      <p>The result is a cyclically reinforcing, multi-tiered narrative ecosystem in which local anxieties, geopolitical messaging, and historical memory intersect. Biological disinformation in Africa is therefore not simply imported; it is continually reshaped through regional insecurities and political realities, and its local consequences are then recycled upward as supposed proof of Western wrongdoing. This dynamic ensures that false narratives remain durable, adaptable and deeply embedded across the continent's information landscape.</p>`
