    body: `
      <p>Latin America has increasingly emerged as a sustained theatre for state-linked biological disinformation. As one of the world's most socio-politically diverse regions, it faces intersecting pressures from entrenched inequality to fragmented governance and persistent public health burdens. These elements create an information environment in which rumours, mistrust and politically charged narratives may readily take root. Recurrent outbreaks of dengue, Zika, chikungunya, COVID-19 and other endemic and emerging diseases place continual strain on national health systems, generating uncertainty that external actors are able to strategically manipulate.</p>
      <p>International cooperation in health security has been essential to strengthening regional disease detection and response. Yet these same partnerships have become recurrent targets of strategic biological disinformation. In much of Latin America, historical sensitivities around sovereignty, foreign military involvement and biomedical research have produced fertile ground for narratives portraying foreign-supported laboratories as malign interventions. These structural conditions make the region highly receptive to the foundational "biolab" narrative that underpins contemporary Russian biological disinformation campaigns.</p>
      <p>While this foundational narrative is global in design, its expression in Latin America reflects distinct regional dynamics. Here it is filtered through legacies of Cold War intervention, extractive political economies, migration crises and deep public distrust of institutions. As a result, the global biolab frame is routinely localised into claims about border contamination, population manipulation, or foreign exploitation of biological resources. Cases ranging from allegations of planned anthrax attacks in the Haiti–Dominican Republic corridor to accusations targeting US-affiliated laboratories in Peru illustrate how a single global template is reinterpreted through local sociopolitical anxieties.</p>
      <p>These dynamics operate through a Matryoshka-style, tiered architecture. At the smallest tier, community-level fears about disease, migration, poverty or foreign presence generate rumours that are emotionally resonant and highly adaptive. These bottom-up narratives do not exist in isolation but are interpreted through the foundational biolab frame circulating at higher levels. At the intermediate tier, national-level actors such as journalists, political intermediaries, activists and sometimes ministries rearticulate these claims within domestic debates about sovereignty, corruption and security. At the outermost tier, international state-linked messaging, most prominently from Russian defence and foreign-policy actors, legitimises these localised stories by integrating them into a broader geopolitical narrative of clandestine US military-biological activity.</p>
      <p>As in Africa and Southeast Asia, these tiers do not operate linearly but in iterative cycles of reinforcement. Local rumours provide the emotive "evidence" that sustains the narrative whilst national reactions supply political meaning and international disinformation networks elevate these fragments into supposed proof of a global pattern. The resulting ecosystem ensures that biological disinformation in Latin America is not merely imported, but continually recycled across levels, making it durable within the region's information landscape.</p>`
